  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the time it takes to fill the entire tank. We know that the tank is being filled at 2 cubic feet per minute, except for the leakage caused by the hole. \n\nLet's first find the volume of the tank:\nVolume = π × r^2 × h = π × 4^2 × 10 = 160π cubic feet.\n\nWe have the tank filled to 5 feet after 10 minutes. So, the volume of water at that time is:\nVolume = π × 4^2 × 5 = 80π cubic feet.\n\nNow, we need to find the rate of leakage. Since the we're not given the exact rate of leakage, we need to find a relationship between the water volume and time. We know that the water level rises to 5 feet after 10 minutes. So, the rate of leakage must correspond to:\nRate of leakage = (160π - 80π) / (10 minutes) = 8π cubic feet per minute.\n\nNext, we need to find the time it takes to fill the entire tank. Let t be the time it takes to fill the tank. The volume of water in the tank after t minutes is:\nVolume = 2t cubic feet.\n\nHowever, we must subtract the leakage that occurs during this time. The leakage rate is proportional to the square root of the water's height. Since the water height is 2t, the leakage rate is:\nRate of leakage = k × √(2t).\n\nWe can find the constant k by using the initial condition that the water volume after 10 minutes is 80π cubic feet, and the leakage rate is 8π cubic feet per minute:\n80π = 2 × 10 + k × √(2 × 10) × 10.\n\nSolving for k, we get:\nk = (80π - 20) / (10√20) = 4π - 2 / (√25) = 4π - 2 / 5 ≈ 11.53.\n\nNow, we can use the equation for the leakage rate to find the time it takes to fill the entire tank:\nVolume = 2t - k × √(2t).\n\nSubstitute the values we know:\n80π = 2t - (4π - 2 / 5) × √(2t).\n\nSolve for t:\nt ≈ 30.81 minutes.\n\nThus, it takes approximately 30.81 minutes to fill the entire tank.",
